# Lesson 4, new exercise: QJT95 opposite A432 at no-trump. Mike's line (2026-11-25): duck the first spade, win the second, then cash the ♦A and lead toward dummy, so the diamond is lost, if at all, to East, who has no spade left. The finesse would lose to West's singleton king.
title: Safer than a finesse
id: L04-12
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.7
student: S
board: 78
dealer: E
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  942    Q7     QJT95  A63
  E  T5     J653   K87    KJ42
  S  A73    AK4    A432   Q87
  W  KQJ86  T982   6      T95

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = 8-9 points, balanced: inviting game
  E Pass
  S 3NT    = A maximum: accept
     not Pass: You have 17, the top of your range. Accept the invitation.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT, partner invited with 2NT, and with 17 you accepted. You are
  declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the
  first trick.

planning:
  winners: 9
  spades: 1 = The ace.
  hearts: 3 = Your ace and king, and dummy's queen.
  diamonds: 4 = Four once the king is gone.
  clubs: 1 = Dummy's ace.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: You'll probably have to lose the lead to the ♦K. What could the defense do then?
play:
  1   W SK  N S2  E S5  S S3*
      note: Nine winners once the ♦K is gone. But when an opponent wins it, West's spades are the danger: once your ♠A is gone, they are all winners. Get ready before you touch the diamonds.
      S3 key: Duck. You have to lose a spade sooner or later, so lose it now. Win the next one, and East may have no spade left to lead back to West.
      S3 not SA: Did you think about ducking? Win now, and whoever gets in with the ♦K may still have a spade to lead to West's winners. Lose the spade you have to lose now.
  2   W SQ  N S4  E ST  S SA
      note: Now win the ace. Both opponents followed twice.
  3   S DA*  W D6  N D5  E D7
      note: Now the diamonds. Cash the ace first.
      DA key: The ace first. If West has a singleton king, it falls. Otherwise you'll lead toward dummy, and only East can win the king.
      DA not *: Did you think about the ace? A finesse loses if West has the king, and West is the one with spades to cash. Cash the ace, then lead toward dummy: you lose a diamond only to East.
  4   S D2  W C5  N D9  E DK
      note: Lead toward dummy. West shows out, so East has the king. Let East take it: East has no spades left.
  5   E H3  S H4  W H8  N HQ
      note: East had no spade to lead. You're safe, and the rest of the diamonds are good.
  6   N DQ  E D8  S D3  W C9
      note: Cash the diamonds.
  7   N DJ  E C2  S D4  W S6
  8   N DT  E C4  S C7  W S8
  9   N H7  E H5  S HA  W H9
      note: Back to your hand for the hearts.
  10  S HK  W HT  N C3  E H6
  11  S C8  W CT  N CA  E CJ
      note: And dummy's ♣A. That's nine tricks.
  12  N C6  E CK  S CQ  W H2
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  13  E HJ  S S7  W SJ  N S9
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: nine tricks once the diamonds were set up, but setting them up
  meant losing the lead to the ♦K. So first you ducked a spade and won the
  second, leaving East with none. Then you cashed the ♦A and led toward
  dummy.

  East won the king but had no spade to lead, so West's spades never came
  into play. A finesse of the queen would have worked here too, but it loses
  whenever West has the king. Your line loses only if West has the king with
  another diamond, and it wins when West's king is a singleton: cashing the
  ace drops it.
